1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,915
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,095 --> 00:00:03,651
You've probably heard the quote.

3
00:00:03,831 --> 00:00:06,342
Compound interest is the eighth wonder of

4
00:00:06,342 --> 00:00:06,954
the world.

5
00:00:07,134 --> 00:00:08,386
He who understands it,

6
00:00:08,386 --> 00:00:08,899
earns it.

7
00:00:09,079 --> 00:00:10,108
He who doesn't,

8
00:00:10,108 --> 00:00:10,658
pays it.

9
00:00:10,838 --> 00:00:13,276
It's usually credited to Albert Einstein.

10
00:00:13,456 --> 00:00:14,675
Here's a fun fact.

11
00:00:14,855 --> 00:00:17,329
There's no good evidence Einstein ever said

12
00:00:17,329 --> 00:00:17,502
it.

13
00:00:17,682 --> 00:00:19,087
But whoever said it was right.

14
00:00:19,267 --> 00:00:22,132
Compound interest is the most powerful force

15
00:00:22,132 --> 00:00:23,435
in personal finance.

16
00:00:23,615 --> 00:00:25,716
It can make ordinary people wealthy,

17
00:00:25,716 --> 00:00:27,934
and it can keep ordinary people broke.

18
00:00:28,114 --> 00:00:30,884
Today, we're going to explain it completely,

19
00:00:30,884 --> 00:00:32,017
in twenty lessons,

20
00:00:32,017 --> 00:00:33,906
so it works for you instead of

21
00:00:33,906 --> 00:00:34,662
against you.

22
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:37,426
Here's compound interest in one picture.

23
00:00:37,606 --> 00:00:39,867
Would you rather have one million dollars

24
00:00:39,867 --> 00:00:41,908
today, or a single penny that doubles

25
00:00:41,908 --> 00:00:43,342
every day for thirty days?

26
00:00:43,522 --> 00:00:44,451
On day thirty,

27
00:00:44,451 --> 00:00:46,640
the penny is worth more than five

28
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:47,701
million dollars.

29
00:00:48,052 --> 00:00:50,550
The penny puzzle shows the strange thing

30
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:51,674
about compounding.

31
00:00:51,854 --> 00:00:53,071
For most of the month,

32
00:00:53,071 --> 00:00:54,454
the penny looks pathetic.

33
00:00:54,634 --> 00:00:55,379
On day ten,

34
00:00:55,379 --> 00:00:57,409
it's worth about five dollars.

35
00:00:57,589 --> 00:00:58,526
On day twenty,

36
00:00:58,526 --> 00:00:59,865
about five thousand.

37
00:01:00,045 --> 00:01:01,229
Then it explodes.

38
00:01:01,409 --> 00:01:03,370
Most of the growth happens at the

39
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:03,905
very end.

40
00:01:04,085 --> 00:01:07,001
That's exactly how compound interest works with

41
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:09,359
real money, just over years instead of

42
00:01:09,359 --> 00:01:09,670
days.

43
00:01:09,850 --> 00:01:12,475
The early years feel slow and almost

44
00:01:12,475 --> 00:01:13,205
pointless.

45
00:01:13,385 --> 00:01:15,324
The later years feel like magic.

46
00:01:15,504 --> 00:01:17,769
And most people quit before the magic

47
00:01:17,769 --> 00:01:18,197
starts.

48
00:01:18,548 --> 00:01:21,601
We'll explain compounding from three angles.

49
00:01:21,781 --> 00:01:23,197
First, the numbers.

50
00:01:23,377 --> 00:01:24,867
How it actually works,

51
00:01:24,867 --> 00:01:26,493
the rule of seventy two,

52
00:01:26,493 --> 00:01:29,608
compounding frequency and the real math behind

53
00:01:29,608 --> 00:01:30,692
famous examples.

54
00:01:30,872 --> 00:01:32,706
Second, the psychology.

55
00:01:32,886 --> 00:01:35,884
Why our brains struggle to understand exponential

56
00:01:35,884 --> 00:01:38,087
growth, and how that costs us money.

57
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:39,893
And third, the playbook.

58
00:01:40,073 --> 00:01:41,904
How to put compounding to work in

59
00:01:41,904 --> 00:01:43,514
your life, and how to stop it

60
00:01:43,514 --> 00:01:44,624
working against you.

61
00:01:44,804 --> 00:01:45,524
Quick note.

62
00:01:45,704 --> 00:01:48,838
Growth examples use stated returns like seven

63
00:01:48,838 --> 00:01:49,396
percent.

64
00:01:49,576 --> 00:01:51,285
Those are illustrations,

65
00:01:51,285 --> 00:01:52,211
not promises.

66
00:01:52,561 --> 00:01:54,302
Let's start at the very beginning.

67
00:01:54,482 --> 00:01:56,515
What is compound interest,

68
00:01:56,515 --> 00:01:57,141
exactly?

69
00:01:57,491 --> 00:01:58,477
Our sources.

70
00:01:58,657 --> 00:02:01,531
The Einstein quote's history comes from quote

71
00:02:01,531 --> 00:02:04,085
researchers like the Quote Investigator,

72
00:02:04,085 --> 00:02:06,065
which found no reliable source.

73
00:02:06,245 --> 00:02:09,841
Benjamin Franklin's bequest comes from historical records

74
00:02:09,841 --> 00:02:12,112
of the Franklin trusts in Boston and

75
00:02:12,112 --> 00:02:12,932
Philadelphia.

76
00:02:13,112 --> 00:02:16,541
Research on how people misunderstand compounding comes

77
00:02:16,541 --> 00:02:19,716
from economists Victor Stango and Jonathan Zinman,

78
00:02:19,716 --> 00:02:22,828
and from financial literacy research by Annamaria

79
00:02:22,828 --> 00:02:24,606
Lusardi and Olivia Mitchell.

80
00:02:24,786 --> 00:02:26,982
Credit card rates come from the Federal

81
00:02:26,982 --> 00:02:27,433
Reserve.

82
00:02:27,613 --> 00:02:28,974
This is education,

83
00:02:28,974 --> 00:02:30,562
not financial advice.

84
00:02:30,912 --> 00:02:31,713
Angle one.

85
00:02:31,893 --> 00:02:32,671
The numbers.

86
00:02:32,851 --> 00:02:35,776
Seven lessons on how compounding really works.

87
00:02:36,127 --> 00:02:37,230
Lesson number one.

88
00:02:37,410 --> 00:02:40,393
The difference between simple and compound interest.

89
00:02:40,744 --> 00:02:43,147
Simple interest is paid only on your

90
00:02:43,147 --> 00:02:44,215
original amount.

91
00:02:44,395 --> 00:02:46,555
Put one thousand dollars in an account

92
00:02:46,555 --> 00:02:48,545
paying ten percent simple interest,

93
00:02:48,545 --> 00:02:50,705
and you earn one hundred dollars every

94
00:02:50,705 --> 00:02:50,989
year.

95
00:02:51,169 --> 00:02:52,219
After ten years,

96
00:02:52,219 --> 00:02:54,188
you have two thousand dollars.

97
00:02:54,368 --> 00:02:57,700
Steady, predictable, and kind of boring.

98
00:02:58,050 --> 00:03:00,811
Compound interest is paid on your original

99
00:03:00,811 --> 00:03:03,638
amount plus all the interest you've already

100
00:03:03,638 --> 00:03:04,098
earned.

101
00:03:04,278 --> 00:03:06,602
The same one thousand dollars at ten

102
00:03:06,602 --> 00:03:09,829
percent compounded yearly becomes one thousand one

103
00:03:09,829 --> 00:03:11,313
hundred after year one,

104
00:03:11,313 --> 00:03:13,508
then one thousand two hundred ten,

105
00:03:13,508 --> 00:03:16,283
then one thousand three hundred thirty one.

106
00:03:16,463 --> 00:03:17,392
After ten years,

107
00:03:17,392 --> 00:03:19,888
it's about two thousand five hundred ninety

108
00:03:19,888 --> 00:03:20,643
four dollars.

109
00:03:20,823 --> 00:03:23,179
Almost six hundred dollars more than simple

110
00:03:23,179 --> 00:03:24,933
interest, without adding a cent.

111
00:03:25,283 --> 00:03:26,764
Over longer periods,

112
00:03:26,764 --> 00:03:28,615
the gap becomes enormous.

113
00:03:28,795 --> 00:03:29,914
After thirty years,

114
00:03:29,914 --> 00:03:32,742
simple interest gives you four thousand dollars.

115
00:03:32,922 --> 00:03:35,803
Compound interest at ten percent gives you

116
00:03:35,803 --> 00:03:37,450
over seventeen thousand.

117
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:39,070
Same starting money,

118
00:03:39,070 --> 00:03:39,789
same rate.

119
00:03:39,969 --> 00:03:41,971
The only difference is that the interest

120
00:03:41,971 --> 00:03:43,522
itself starts earning interest.

121
00:03:43,872 --> 00:03:45,102
Remember it this way.

122
00:03:45,282 --> 00:03:47,488
Simple interest is a staircase.

123
00:03:47,668 --> 00:03:49,909
Compound interest is a snowball.

124
00:03:50,089 --> 00:03:52,638
The staircase climbs the same step every

125
00:03:52,638 --> 00:03:52,957
year.

126
00:03:53,137 --> 00:03:55,786
The snowball picks up more snow the

127
00:03:55,786 --> 00:03:56,921
bigger it gets.

128
00:03:57,272 --> 00:03:58,375
Lesson number two.

129
00:03:58,555 --> 00:03:59,983
The rule of seventy two.

130
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:02,995
Here's the most useful shortcut in personal

131
00:04:02,995 --> 00:04:03,490
finance.

132
00:04:03,670 --> 00:04:06,137
Divide seventy two by your yearly return,

133
00:04:06,137 --> 00:04:08,183
and you get roughly how many years

134
00:04:08,183 --> 00:04:09,928
it takes for money to double.

135
00:04:10,108 --> 00:04:11,353
At six percent,

136
00:04:11,353 --> 00:04:12,929
about twelve years.

137
00:04:13,109 --> 00:04:14,358
At eight percent,

138
00:04:14,358 --> 00:04:15,606
about nine years.

139
00:04:15,786 --> 00:04:17,304
At twelve percent,

140
00:04:17,304 --> 00:04:18,653
about six years.

141
00:04:18,833 --> 00:04:19,933
It's an estimate,

142
00:04:19,933 --> 00:04:22,781
but it's remarkably close for typical rates.

143
00:04:23,131 --> 00:04:25,441
The rule works for anything that grows

144
00:04:25,441 --> 00:04:26,474
at a steady rate.

145
00:04:26,654 --> 00:04:29,232
Investments, debt, even inflation.

146
00:04:29,412 --> 00:04:31,265
At three percent inflation,

147
00:04:31,265 --> 00:04:34,079
prices double in about twenty four years.

148
00:04:34,259 --> 00:04:37,198
At twenty four percent credit card interest,

149
00:04:37,198 --> 00:04:39,669
an unpaid balance can double in about

150
00:04:39,669 --> 00:04:40,470
three years.

151
00:04:40,821 --> 00:04:42,504
Now think in doublings.

152
00:04:42,684 --> 00:04:43,897
At seven percent,

153
00:04:43,897 --> 00:04:46,608
money doubles roughly every ten years.

154
00:04:46,788 --> 00:04:49,291
Ten thousand dollars at age thirty could

155
00:04:49,291 --> 00:04:51,669
become about twenty thousand at forty,

156
00:04:51,669 --> 00:04:53,170
forty thousand at fifty,

157
00:04:53,170 --> 00:04:54,985
and eighty thousand at sixty.

158
00:04:55,165 --> 00:04:57,589
Each decade, the jump is bigger than

159
00:04:57,589 --> 00:04:58,195
the last.

160
00:04:58,545 --> 00:04:59,404
Try this.

161
00:04:59,584 --> 00:05:01,964
Take your savings account rate and divide

162
00:05:01,964 --> 00:05:03,009
seventy two by it.

163
00:05:03,189 --> 00:05:04,955
If the answer is fifty years or

164
00:05:04,955 --> 00:05:06,950
more, your money is barely growing.

165
00:05:07,130 --> 00:05:09,105
Then try it with your credit card

166
00:05:09,105 --> 00:05:10,961
rate, and see how fast debt can

167
00:05:10,961 --> 00:05:11,380
double.

168
00:05:11,730 --> 00:05:13,320
Here's the doubling in action.

169
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:16,020
Ten thousand dollars at a seven percent

170
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:17,442
average yearly return,

171
00:05:17,442 --> 00:05:19,897
roughly doubles about every ten years.

172
00:05:20,077 --> 00:05:21,212
After thirty years,

173
00:05:21,212 --> 00:05:23,542
it's about eighty one thousand dollars,

174
00:05:23,542 --> 00:05:25,395
without adding a single dollar.

175
00:05:25,745 --> 00:05:27,068
Lesson number three.

176
00:05:27,248 --> 00:05:29,443
How often interest compounds matters,

177
00:05:29,443 --> 00:05:29,976
a little.

178
00:05:30,327 --> 00:05:32,406
Interest can compound yearly,

179
00:05:32,406 --> 00:05:33,984
monthly or even daily.

180
00:05:34,164 --> 00:05:35,904
The more often it compounds,

181
00:05:35,904 --> 00:05:37,706
the faster the balance grows,

182
00:05:37,706 --> 00:05:40,689
because interest starts earning interest sooner.

183
00:05:40,869 --> 00:05:44,147
Twelve percent compounded monthly is slightly more

184
00:05:44,147 --> 00:05:46,639
than twelve percent compounded yearly.

185
00:05:46,989 --> 00:05:49,032
That's why banks show two numbers.

186
00:05:49,212 --> 00:05:51,206
The annual percentage rate,

187
00:05:51,206 --> 00:05:53,717
or APR, is the simple yearly rate.

188
00:05:53,897 --> 00:05:55,962
The annual percentage yield,

189
00:05:55,962 --> 00:05:59,133
or APY, includes the effect of compounding.

190
00:05:59,313 --> 00:06:01,762
On savings, compare APYs.

191
00:06:01,942 --> 00:06:04,748
On debt, remember that the effective cost

192
00:06:04,748 --> 00:06:06,869
can be a little higher than the

193
00:06:06,869 --> 00:06:09,059
APR, because interest compounds.

194
00:06:09,410 --> 00:06:12,322
Most credit cards calculate interest daily,

195
00:06:12,322 --> 00:06:14,421
using a daily rate based on the

196
00:06:14,421 --> 00:06:14,692
APR.

197
00:06:14,872 --> 00:06:16,850
So a card at twenty four percent

198
00:06:16,850 --> 00:06:19,384
charges roughly six and a half hundredths

199
00:06:19,384 --> 00:06:21,361
of a percent every single day on

200
00:06:21,361 --> 00:06:24,081
your balance, and that interest becomes part

201
00:06:24,081 --> 00:06:25,008
of the balance.

202
00:06:25,358 --> 00:06:28,501
In practice, frequency matters much less than

203
00:06:28,501 --> 00:06:29,479
rate and time.

204
00:06:29,659 --> 00:06:32,155
Don't obsess over daily versus monthly.

205
00:06:32,335 --> 00:06:34,482
Focus on getting a good rate on

206
00:06:34,482 --> 00:06:36,422
savings, a low rate on debt,

207
00:06:36,422 --> 00:06:38,430
and as much time as possible.

208
00:06:38,781 --> 00:06:39,930
Lesson number four.

209
00:06:40,110 --> 00:06:43,059
Regular contributions supercharge compounding.

210
00:06:43,409 --> 00:06:45,893
A single deposit compounds nicely.

211
00:06:46,073 --> 00:06:48,207
But adding money every month is where

212
00:06:48,207 --> 00:06:49,707
compounding really shines.

213
00:06:49,887 --> 00:06:52,252
Each new deposit starts its own little

214
00:06:52,252 --> 00:06:52,813
snowball.

215
00:06:52,993 --> 00:06:53,916
Over the years,

216
00:06:53,916 --> 00:06:56,685
you're rolling hundreds of snowballs at once.

217
00:06:57,035 --> 00:06:59,181
Two hundred dollars a month for thirty

218
00:06:59,181 --> 00:07:01,383
years at a seven percent average yearly

219
00:07:01,383 --> 00:07:03,698
return grows to roughly two hundred forty

220
00:07:03,698 --> 00:07:04,941
four thousand dollars.

221
00:07:05,121 --> 00:07:07,118
You contribute seventy two thousand.

222
00:07:07,298 --> 00:07:09,792
The other one hundred seventy two thousand

223
00:07:09,792 --> 00:07:10,386
is growth.

224
00:07:10,736 --> 00:07:12,642
Notice that in the early years,

225
00:07:12,642 --> 00:07:15,287
your contributions are most of the balance.

226
00:07:15,467 --> 00:07:18,234
Somewhere around year fifteen or twenty in

227
00:07:18,234 --> 00:07:21,068
this example, yearly growth starts to rival

228
00:07:21,068 --> 00:07:22,584
what you add each year.

229
00:07:22,764 --> 00:07:24,711
After that, the money is doing more

230
00:07:24,711 --> 00:07:25,712
work than you are.

231
00:07:26,062 --> 00:07:26,887
Try this.

232
00:07:27,067 --> 00:07:30,064
Set up an automatic monthly investment,

233
00:07:30,064 --> 00:07:30,910
even small.

234
00:07:31,090 --> 00:07:32,671
Then look up the year when your

235
00:07:32,671 --> 00:07:35,374
yearly growth might exceed your yearly contributions.

236
00:07:35,554 --> 00:07:37,725
That's a milestone worth celebrating.

237
00:07:38,075 --> 00:07:40,420
Two hundred dollars a month for thirty

238
00:07:40,420 --> 00:07:42,889
years, at a seven percent average yearly

239
00:07:42,889 --> 00:07:45,482
return, grows to roughly two hundred forty

240
00:07:45,482 --> 00:07:46,840
four thousand dollars.

241
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:49,714
Total contributed, seventy two thousand.

242
00:07:50,063 --> 00:07:51,550
Lesson number five.

243
00:07:51,730 --> 00:07:54,377
Benjamin Franklin's two hundred year experiment.

244
00:07:54,726 --> 00:07:57,777
When Benjamin Franklin died in seventeen ninety,

245
00:07:57,777 --> 00:08:00,001
he left one thousand pounds each to

246
00:08:00,001 --> 00:08:02,415
the cities of Boston and Philadelphia,

247
00:08:02,415 --> 00:08:04,830
with instructions to lend the money to

248
00:08:04,830 --> 00:08:07,245
young tradesmen at interest and let it

249
00:08:07,245 --> 00:08:08,960
grow for two hundred years.

250
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:11,857
It was a deliberate demonstration of compounding.

251
00:08:12,206 --> 00:08:14,427
Part of the money was released after

252
00:08:14,427 --> 00:08:15,537
one hundred years,

253
00:08:15,537 --> 00:08:16,955
as Franklin instructed.

254
00:08:17,135 --> 00:08:18,424
The rest kept growing.

255
00:08:18,604 --> 00:08:19,864
By nineteen ninety,

256
00:08:19,864 --> 00:08:22,253
the funds had grown into millions of

257
00:08:22,253 --> 00:08:22,783
dollars.

258
00:08:22,963 --> 00:08:25,820
Boston's fund was worth around five million,

259
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:28,286
and Philadelphia's around two million,

260
00:08:28,286 --> 00:08:30,428
according to historical accounts.

261
00:08:30,779 --> 00:08:32,727
The returns weren't spectacular,

262
00:08:32,727 --> 00:08:35,406
and the funds faced management problems over

263
00:08:35,406 --> 00:08:36,258
the centuries.

264
00:08:36,438 --> 00:08:37,960
But even modest growth,

265
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:39,547
given two hundred years,

266
00:08:39,547 --> 00:08:41,466
turned a gift into a fortune.

267
00:08:41,646 --> 00:08:43,201
Time can make up for a lot.

268
00:08:43,552 --> 00:08:44,771
The lesson for us.

269
00:08:44,951 --> 00:08:46,680
We don't have two hundred years.

270
00:08:46,860 --> 00:08:48,753
But we might have forty or fifty.

271
00:08:48,933 --> 00:08:51,429
That's plenty of time for compounding to

272
00:08:51,429 --> 00:08:52,927
do something remarkable.

273
00:08:53,277 --> 00:08:54,670
Lesson number six.

274
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:57,556
Why most of Warren Buffett's fortune came

275
00:08:57,556 --> 00:08:58,414
late in life.

276
00:08:58,763 --> 00:09:00,903
Warren Buffett is one of the most

277
00:09:00,903 --> 00:09:02,978
successful investors in history.

278
00:09:03,158 --> 00:09:05,584
But as writer Morgan Housel points out

279
00:09:05,584 --> 00:09:07,308
in The Psychology of Money,

280
00:09:07,308 --> 00:09:09,863
the vast majority of Buffett's net worth

281
00:09:09,863 --> 00:09:12,864
was accumulated after his sixty fifth birthday.

282
00:09:13,214 --> 00:09:16,009
Buffett started investing as a child and

283
00:09:16,009 --> 00:09:16,987
never stopped.

284
00:09:17,167 --> 00:09:18,518
His skill matters,

285
00:09:18,518 --> 00:09:19,268
of course.

286
00:09:19,448 --> 00:09:21,752
But Housel argues the real secret is

287
00:09:21,752 --> 00:09:22,072
time.

288
00:09:22,252 --> 00:09:24,637
Buffett has been compounding for more than

289
00:09:24,637 --> 00:09:25,375
eighty years.

290
00:09:25,726 --> 00:09:28,113
Housel makes the point that if Buffett

291
00:09:28,113 --> 00:09:30,375
had started at thirty and retired at

292
00:09:30,375 --> 00:09:31,820
sixty, he'd be wealthy,

293
00:09:31,820 --> 00:09:33,516
but nowhere near as famous.

294
00:09:33,696 --> 00:09:35,663
The extra decades at both ends made

295
00:09:35,663 --> 00:09:36,505
the difference.

296
00:09:36,856 --> 00:09:38,107
For regular people,

297
00:09:38,107 --> 00:09:39,491
the lesson is simple.

298
00:09:39,671 --> 00:09:41,516
Start as early as you can,

299
00:09:41,516 --> 00:09:43,787
and stay invested as long as you

300
00:09:43,787 --> 00:09:44,071
can.

301
00:09:44,422 --> 00:09:45,768
Lesson number seven.

302
00:09:45,948 --> 00:09:48,204
Compounding works against you when you owe

303
00:09:48,204 --> 00:09:48,526
money.

304
00:09:48,876 --> 00:09:51,282
Everything we've said about growth applies to

305
00:09:51,282 --> 00:09:52,138
debt in reverse.

306
00:09:52,318 --> 00:09:55,030
The Federal Reserve reports average credit card

307
00:09:55,030 --> 00:09:57,685
rates above twenty percent for accounts paying

308
00:09:57,685 --> 00:09:58,204
interest.

309
00:09:58,384 --> 00:09:59,496
At those rates,

310
00:09:59,496 --> 00:10:01,647
unpaid balances grow quickly.

311
00:10:01,997 --> 00:10:04,194
If you only make the minimum payment,

312
00:10:04,194 --> 00:10:05,917
it can take many years to pay

313
00:10:05,917 --> 00:10:06,749
off a balance,

314
00:10:06,749 --> 00:10:08,590
and the total interest can be a

315
00:10:08,590 --> 00:10:10,669
large share of the original amount.

316
00:10:10,849 --> 00:10:13,377
Credit card statements are required to show

317
00:10:13,377 --> 00:10:13,670
this.

318
00:10:14,021 --> 00:10:15,696
This is why the quote ends the

319
00:10:15,696 --> 00:10:16,366
way it does.

320
00:10:16,546 --> 00:10:17,856
He who understands it,

321
00:10:17,856 --> 00:10:18,392
earns it.

322
00:10:18,572 --> 00:10:19,556
He who doesn't,

323
00:10:19,556 --> 00:10:20,081
pays it.

324
00:10:20,431 --> 00:10:21,220
Try this.

325
00:10:21,400 --> 00:10:23,495
Find the minimum payment warning box on

326
00:10:23,495 --> 00:10:24,570
your card statement.

327
00:10:24,750 --> 00:10:26,665
It shows how long payoff takes at

328
00:10:26,665 --> 00:10:27,362
the minimum.

329
00:10:27,712 --> 00:10:28,594
That's angle one.

330
00:10:28,774 --> 00:10:30,500
Simple versus compound,

331
00:10:30,500 --> 00:10:32,300
the rule of seventy two,

332
00:10:32,300 --> 00:10:34,776
frequency, monthly contributions,

333
00:10:34,776 --> 00:10:38,228
Franklin's experiment, Buffett's late fortune,

334
00:10:38,228 --> 00:10:39,803
and compounding debt.

335
00:10:39,983 --> 00:10:41,214
Now angle two.

336
00:10:41,394 --> 00:10:42,520
The psychology.

337
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:44,987
Why our brains get this so wrong.

338
00:10:45,338 --> 00:10:48,035
Humans evolved in a world of straight

339
00:10:48,035 --> 00:10:48,472
lines.

340
00:10:48,652 --> 00:10:50,275
If you walk twice as far,

341
00:10:50,275 --> 00:10:52,158
it takes about twice as long.

342
00:10:52,338 --> 00:10:54,467
If you gather twice as many berries,

343
00:10:54,467 --> 00:10:56,123
you have twice as much food.

344
00:10:56,303 --> 00:10:59,452
Exponential growth is unnatural for our brains,

345
00:10:59,452 --> 00:11:03,002
and that's why compounding surprises nearly everyone.

346
00:11:03,182 --> 00:11:04,033
In this angle,

347
00:11:04,033 --> 00:11:06,405
we'll look at seven ways our psychology

348
00:11:06,405 --> 00:11:08,289
gets in the way of compounding,

349
00:11:08,289 --> 00:11:10,113
and what to do about each one.

350
00:11:10,464 --> 00:11:11,625
Lesson number eight.

351
00:11:11,805 --> 00:11:13,755
Exponential growth bias.

352
00:11:14,105 --> 00:11:17,675
Economists Victor Stango and Jonathan Zinman studied

353
00:11:17,675 --> 00:11:20,490
what they called exponential growth bias.

354
00:11:20,670 --> 00:11:23,054
When people are asked to estimate how

355
00:11:23,054 --> 00:11:25,567
something grows at a steady percentage,

356
00:11:25,567 --> 00:11:27,886
they tend to imagine a straight line

357
00:11:27,886 --> 00:11:29,110
instead of a curve.

358
00:11:29,290 --> 00:11:32,470
So they badly underestimate long term growth,

359
00:11:32,470 --> 00:11:34,236
on both savings and debt.

360
00:11:34,587 --> 00:11:37,400
Their research found that households with stronger

361
00:11:37,400 --> 00:11:39,313
bias tended to borrow more at high

362
00:11:39,313 --> 00:11:40,438
rates and save less.

363
00:11:40,618 --> 00:11:41,489
That makes sense.

364
00:11:41,669 --> 00:11:43,727
If you think a credit card balance

365
00:11:43,727 --> 00:11:45,906
grows slowly, borrowing feels cheap.

366
00:11:46,086 --> 00:11:48,426
If you think savings grow slowly,

367
00:11:48,426 --> 00:11:50,057
saving feels pointless.

368
00:11:50,237 --> 00:11:53,543
Underestimating compounding costs money on both sides

369
00:11:53,543 --> 00:11:54,417
of the ledger.

370
00:11:54,767 --> 00:11:57,084
The penny puzzle from the beginning works

371
00:11:57,084 --> 00:11:58,667
because it shocks this bias.

372
00:11:58,847 --> 00:12:01,651
Almost nobody guesses that a penny doubled

373
00:12:01,651 --> 00:12:03,921
for thirty days ends up above five

374
00:12:03,921 --> 00:12:04,989
million dollars.

375
00:12:05,169 --> 00:12:08,350
Our intuition simply isn't built for doubling.

376
00:12:08,530 --> 00:12:10,144
That's not a personal failing.

377
00:12:10,324 --> 00:12:12,172
It's how human brains work,

378
00:12:12,172 --> 00:12:14,500
which is why we need tools instead

379
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:15,595
of gut feelings.

380
00:12:15,945 --> 00:12:16,792
Try this.

381
00:12:16,972 --> 00:12:20,210
Before calculating, guess what one hundred dollars

382
00:12:20,210 --> 00:12:22,347
a month becomes in forty years at

383
00:12:22,347 --> 00:12:23,253
seven percent.

384
00:12:23,433 --> 00:12:24,559
Write your guess down.

385
00:12:24,739 --> 00:12:26,283
Then check it with a calculator.

386
00:12:26,463 --> 00:12:28,619
The real answer is roughly two hundred

387
00:12:28,619 --> 00:12:29,923
sixty thousand dollars.

388
00:12:30,103 --> 00:12:32,110
Most people guess far too low,

389
00:12:32,110 --> 00:12:33,917
and that gap is the cost of

390
00:12:33,917 --> 00:12:35,455
straight line thinking.

391
00:12:35,806 --> 00:12:37,187
Lesson number nine.

392
00:12:37,367 --> 00:12:38,249
The boring middle.

393
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:40,884
In the first years of investing,

394
00:12:40,884 --> 00:12:41,955
growth is tiny.

395
00:12:42,135 --> 00:12:44,364
After five years of one hundred dollars

396
00:12:44,364 --> 00:12:45,793
a month at seven percent,

397
00:12:45,793 --> 00:12:48,365
you might have around seven thousand dollars,

398
00:12:48,365 --> 00:12:49,965
and most of that is your own

399
00:12:49,965 --> 00:12:50,308
money.

400
00:12:50,488 --> 00:12:52,278
The growth part might be about a

401
00:12:52,278 --> 00:12:53,228
thousand dollars.

402
00:12:53,408 --> 00:12:56,496
It honestly feels like nothing is happening.

403
00:12:56,847 --> 00:12:58,588
This is where many people quit.

404
00:12:58,768 --> 00:13:00,248
They stop contributing,

405
00:13:00,248 --> 00:13:02,628
cash out for something more exciting,

406
00:13:02,628 --> 00:13:05,072
or switch strategies every few months.

407
00:13:05,252 --> 00:13:07,666
They leave right before the curve starts

408
00:13:07,666 --> 00:13:10,020
bending upward, which means they do all

409
00:13:10,020 --> 00:13:11,951
the hard, slow work and miss the

410
00:13:11,951 --> 00:13:12,857
rewarding part.

411
00:13:13,207 --> 00:13:14,635
Think back to the penny.

412
00:13:14,815 --> 00:13:15,640
On day ten,

413
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:17,891
it's worth about five dollars.

414
00:13:18,071 --> 00:13:19,008
On day twenty,

415
00:13:19,008 --> 00:13:20,347
about five thousand.

416
00:13:20,527 --> 00:13:21,397
On day thirty,

417
00:13:21,397 --> 00:13:22,826
more than five million.

418
00:13:23,006 --> 00:13:25,011
If you'd judged the penny on day

419
00:13:25,011 --> 00:13:27,266
ten, you'd have called it a failure.

420
00:13:27,446 --> 00:13:29,405
The boring middle isn't a sign that

421
00:13:29,405 --> 00:13:30,860
compounding isn't working.

422
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:33,357
It's a normal part of how compounding

423
00:13:33,357 --> 00:13:33,733
works.

424
00:13:34,084 --> 00:13:34,943
Try this.

425
00:13:35,123 --> 00:13:36,860
For the first five years,

426
00:13:36,860 --> 00:13:39,223
measure your success by the habit,

427
00:13:39,223 --> 00:13:40,336
not the balance.

428
00:13:40,516 --> 00:13:43,488
Did the automatic investment happen every month?

429
00:13:43,668 --> 00:13:44,987
Did you raise it when you got

430
00:13:44,987 --> 00:13:45,351
a raise?

431
00:13:45,531 --> 00:13:47,177
If yes, you're winning,

432
00:13:47,177 --> 00:13:49,467
even if the balance looks small.

433
00:13:49,647 --> 00:13:52,025
The balance will take care of itself

434
00:13:52,025 --> 00:13:52,422
later.

435
00:13:52,772 --> 00:13:54,118
Lesson number ten.

436
00:13:54,298 --> 00:13:57,607
Impatience leads people to chase faster returns.

437
00:13:57,958 --> 00:14:01,052
Because normal compounding is slow at first,

438
00:14:01,052 --> 00:14:02,880
people look for shortcuts.

439
00:14:03,060 --> 00:14:05,037
Hot stocks, trendy coins,

440
00:14:05,037 --> 00:14:08,122
leveraged bets, get rich quick schemes.

441
00:14:08,302 --> 00:14:10,883
The promise of fast returns is especially

442
00:14:10,883 --> 00:14:13,968
tempting when steady growth feels painfully slow.

443
00:14:14,148 --> 00:14:16,185
And social media is full of people

444
00:14:16,185 --> 00:14:17,563
showing off their wins,

445
00:14:17,563 --> 00:14:19,001
but rarely their losses.

446
00:14:19,351 --> 00:14:21,489
Here's the math that makes chasing so

447
00:14:21,489 --> 00:14:22,067
dangerous.

448
00:14:22,247 --> 00:14:24,383
Big losses destroy compounding.

449
00:14:24,563 --> 00:14:25,680
Lose fifty percent,

450
00:14:25,680 --> 00:14:27,679
and you need a one hundred percent

451
00:14:27,679 --> 00:14:29,442
gain just to get back to where

452
00:14:29,442 --> 00:14:30,148
you started.

453
00:14:30,328 --> 00:14:31,512
Lose eighty percent,

454
00:14:31,512 --> 00:14:33,584
and you need a four hundred percent

455
00:14:33,584 --> 00:14:33,881
gain.

456
00:14:34,061 --> 00:14:36,746
Avoiding large losses matters just as much

457
00:14:36,746 --> 00:14:38,280
as earning good returns,

458
00:14:38,280 --> 00:14:38,983
maybe more.

459
00:14:39,334 --> 00:14:42,211
That's why so many successful long term

460
00:14:42,211 --> 00:14:44,203
investors emphasize steady,

461
00:14:44,203 --> 00:14:47,449
diversified investing over chasing big wins.

462
00:14:47,629 --> 00:14:50,709
A portfolio that grows steadily without huge

463
00:14:50,709 --> 00:14:53,089
crashes often ends up ahead of one

464
00:14:53,089 --> 00:14:54,839
that soars and collapses.

465
00:14:55,019 --> 00:14:58,247
Slow and steady compounding tends to beat

466
00:14:58,247 --> 00:14:59,349
boom and bust.

467
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:00,501
Try this.

468
00:15:00,681 --> 00:15:03,592
Keep your core long term investments boring

469
00:15:03,592 --> 00:15:06,300
and diversified, like broad index funds.

470
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:08,468
If you really want to speculate,

471
00:15:08,468 --> 00:15:10,457
set aside a small amount you can

472
00:15:10,457 --> 00:15:12,073
afford to lose completely,

473
00:15:12,073 --> 00:15:13,750
and never add to it after a

474
00:15:13,750 --> 00:15:14,061
loss.

475
00:15:14,412 --> 00:15:15,503
Quick pause.

476
00:15:15,683 --> 00:15:18,254
If compounding is finally clicking for you,

477
00:15:18,254 --> 00:15:20,466
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

478
00:15:20,646 --> 00:15:22,540
It's free, and it helps us keep

479
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:24,617
explaining money in plain English.

480
00:15:24,797 --> 00:15:26,527
Now, lesson eleven.

481
00:15:26,877 --> 00:15:28,246
Lesson number eleven.

482
00:15:28,426 --> 00:15:30,830
Present bias works against compounding.

483
00:15:31,180 --> 00:15:33,042
Compounding rewards patience,

484
00:15:33,042 --> 00:15:35,225
but our brains are wired to prefer

485
00:15:35,225 --> 00:15:36,381
rewards right now.

486
00:15:36,561 --> 00:15:39,255
Spending one hundred dollars today on something

487
00:15:39,255 --> 00:15:41,319
fun feels much more real than having

488
00:15:41,319 --> 00:15:43,898
several hundred dollars more in thirty years.

489
00:15:44,078 --> 00:15:46,210
Economists call this present bias,

490
00:15:46,210 --> 00:15:48,467
and it's one of the strongest forces

491
00:15:48,467 --> 00:15:49,721
in personal finance.

492
00:15:50,071 --> 00:15:53,334
That's exactly why automatic investing works so

493
00:15:53,334 --> 00:15:53,681
well.

494
00:15:53,861 --> 00:15:55,210
You make the decision once,

495
00:15:55,210 --> 00:15:57,158
when you're calm and thinking about the

496
00:15:57,158 --> 00:15:57,658
long term.

497
00:15:57,838 --> 00:16:00,428
After that, the money moves before present

498
00:16:00,428 --> 00:16:01,785
bias ever gets a vote.

499
00:16:01,965 --> 00:16:03,728
You don't have to win the battle

500
00:16:03,728 --> 00:16:05,270
with temptation every month,

501
00:16:05,270 --> 00:16:06,702
because there's no battle.

502
00:16:07,052 --> 00:16:09,745
Picturing your future self also helps.

503
00:16:09,925 --> 00:16:12,691
Research by Hal Hershfield and colleagues found

504
00:16:12,691 --> 00:16:15,280
that people who viewed digitally aged images

505
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:17,576
of themselves were more willing to save

506
00:16:17,576 --> 00:16:18,458
for the future.

507
00:16:18,638 --> 00:16:20,722
When the future version of you feels

508
00:16:20,722 --> 00:16:21,822
like a real person,

509
00:16:21,822 --> 00:16:24,079
saving feels less like losing money and

510
00:16:24,079 --> 00:16:26,452
more like helping someone you care about.

511
00:16:26,802 --> 00:16:27,545
Try this.

512
00:16:27,725 --> 00:16:30,183
Write down exactly what your investments are

513
00:16:30,183 --> 00:16:30,406
for.

514
00:16:30,586 --> 00:16:31,834
Not just retirement,

515
00:16:31,834 --> 00:16:33,268
but something specific.

516
00:16:33,448 --> 00:16:35,445
Freedom to leave a job you dislike.

517
00:16:35,625 --> 00:16:36,763
A paid off home.

518
00:16:36,943 --> 00:16:38,580
Retiring at sixty.

519
00:16:38,760 --> 00:16:41,171
Specific goals make the future feel close

520
00:16:41,171 --> 00:16:42,348
enough to fight for.

521
00:16:42,698 --> 00:16:44,021
Lesson number twelve.

522
00:16:44,201 --> 00:16:47,011
Interrupting compounding is very expensive.

523
00:16:47,361 --> 00:16:51,142
Charlie Munger, Warren Buffett's longtime business partner,

524
00:16:51,142 --> 00:16:52,039
put it simply.

525
00:16:52,219 --> 00:16:54,477
The first rule of compounding is never

526
00:16:54,477 --> 00:16:56,260
to interrupt it unnecessarily.

527
00:16:56,440 --> 00:16:57,646
Cashing out early,

528
00:16:57,646 --> 00:16:59,857
stopping contributions for years,

529
00:16:59,857 --> 00:17:02,202
or panic selling during a crash all

530
00:17:02,202 --> 00:17:03,274
break the chain,

531
00:17:03,274 --> 00:17:05,418
and the chain is where the magic

532
00:17:05,418 --> 00:17:05,820
lives.

533
00:17:06,171 --> 00:17:09,236
Early withdrawals from retirement accounts are a

534
00:17:09,236 --> 00:17:10,513
common interruption.

535
00:17:10,693 --> 00:17:13,636
Depending on the account and your situation,

536
00:17:13,636 --> 00:17:17,114
withdrawing before retirement age can trigger income

537
00:17:17,114 --> 00:17:18,452
taxes and a penalty,

538
00:17:18,452 --> 00:17:20,927
and the money withdrawn loses all its

539
00:17:20,927 --> 00:17:22,198
future compounding.

540
00:17:22,378 --> 00:17:24,985
A ten thousand dollar withdrawal at thirty

541
00:17:24,985 --> 00:17:27,282
could cost far more than ten thousand

542
00:17:27,282 --> 00:17:28,648
dollars by retirement.

543
00:17:28,998 --> 00:17:30,646
Job changes are another.

544
00:17:30,826 --> 00:17:32,362
When people leave a job,

545
00:17:32,362 --> 00:17:34,346
many cash out their old four oh

546
00:17:34,346 --> 00:17:36,458
one k instead of rolling it over,

547
00:17:36,458 --> 00:17:38,825
especially when the balance is small.

548
00:17:39,005 --> 00:17:40,584
It feels like free money.

549
00:17:40,764 --> 00:17:42,372
But it often means taxes,

550
00:17:42,372 --> 00:17:45,331
penalties, and starting the snowball over from

551
00:17:45,331 --> 00:17:45,652
zero.

552
00:17:46,003 --> 00:17:46,780
Try this.

553
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:48,311
If you change jobs,

554
00:17:48,311 --> 00:17:51,153
roll your old retirement account into an

555
00:17:51,153 --> 00:17:53,427
IRA or your new employer's plan.

556
00:17:53,607 --> 00:17:55,372
And keep an emergency fund,

557
00:17:55,372 --> 00:17:57,857
so a surprise expense never forces you

558
00:17:57,857 --> 00:17:59,099
to break the chain.

559
00:17:59,449 --> 00:18:00,993
Lesson number thirteen.

560
00:18:01,173 --> 00:18:03,495
Most people misunderstand compounding.

561
00:18:03,845 --> 00:18:07,405
Economists Annamaria Lusardi and Olivia Mitchell created

562
00:18:07,405 --> 00:18:10,776
three simple questions to measure financial literacy.

563
00:18:10,956 --> 00:18:13,314
One asks about compound interest,

564
00:18:13,314 --> 00:18:14,744
one about inflation,

565
00:18:14,744 --> 00:18:16,888
and one about diversification.

566
00:18:17,068 --> 00:18:18,910
In surveys over many years,

567
00:18:18,910 --> 00:18:21,706
a large share of American adults couldn't

568
00:18:21,706 --> 00:18:23,547
answer all three correctly.

569
00:18:23,897 --> 00:18:26,564
Their research found that people who answered

570
00:18:26,564 --> 00:18:28,817
correctly were more likely to plan for

571
00:18:28,817 --> 00:18:31,722
retirement, invest and avoid high cost borrowing.

572
00:18:31,902 --> 00:18:32,951
In other words,

573
00:18:32,951 --> 00:18:36,728
understanding concepts like compounding is linked with

574
00:18:36,728 --> 00:18:38,546
better financial outcomes,

575
00:18:38,546 --> 00:18:40,505
not just better test scores.

