00:28:07,411 --> 00:28:09,902
to roughly three hundred sixty six thousand

861
00:28:09,902 --> 00:28:10,365
dollars.

862
00:28:10,545 --> 00:28:13,264
That's still a very real retirement cushion,

863
00:28:13,264 --> 00:28:14,562
but notice something.

864
00:28:14,742 --> 00:28:16,805
Waiting just ten years cut the final

865
00:28:16,805 --> 00:28:18,237
number by more than half.

866
00:28:18,417 --> 00:28:20,031
That's the cost of delay,

867
00:28:20,031 --> 00:28:21,064
in one sentence.

868
00:28:21,414 --> 00:28:22,888
Start at forty five.

869
00:28:23,068 --> 00:28:25,441
Twenty years grows to roughly one hundred

870
00:28:25,441 --> 00:28:27,004
fifty six thousand dollars.

871
00:28:27,184 --> 00:28:28,503
Start at fifty five,

872
00:28:28,503 --> 00:28:30,878
and ten years grows to roughly fifty

873
00:28:30,878 --> 00:28:31,735
two thousand.

874
00:28:31,915 --> 00:28:34,342
Less dramatic, but still meaningful.

875
00:28:34,522 --> 00:28:36,607
And people who start later often earn

876
00:28:36,607 --> 00:28:38,523
more, so they can invest more each

877
00:28:38,523 --> 00:28:40,101
month to make up some of the

878
00:28:40,101 --> 00:28:40,327
gap.

879
00:28:40,677 --> 00:28:42,116
Here's the catch up math.

880
00:28:42,296 --> 00:28:44,212
If you start at forty and invest

881
00:28:44,212 --> 00:28:46,547
one thousand dollars a month for twenty

882
00:28:46,547 --> 00:28:48,762
five years at the same seven percent,

883
00:28:48,762 --> 00:28:51,217
you could reach roughly eight hundred ten

884
00:28:51,217 --> 00:28:52,234
thousand dollars.

885
00:28:52,414 --> 00:28:54,388
So a late start isn't hopeless.

886
00:28:54,568 --> 00:28:56,537
It just means time is scarcer,

887
00:28:56,537 --> 00:28:59,293
so your contributions have to work harder.

888
00:28:59,644 --> 00:29:01,506
And in the United States,

889
00:29:01,506 --> 00:29:04,784
people fifty and older are generally allowed

890
00:29:04,784 --> 00:29:08,583
extra catch up contributions in retirement accounts

891
00:29:08,583 --> 00:29:10,743
like four oh one ks and IRAs.

892
00:29:10,923 --> 00:29:12,154
The limits change,

893
00:29:12,154 --> 00:29:14,549
so check the current numbers on the

894
00:29:14,549 --> 00:29:15,369
IRS website.

895
00:29:15,549 --> 00:29:17,725
The rules were written with late starters

896
00:29:17,725 --> 00:29:18,150
in mind.

897
00:29:18,500 --> 00:29:19,312
Question one.

898
00:29:19,492 --> 00:29:21,211
Do savings accounts compound?

899
00:29:21,391 --> 00:29:23,841
Yes, most pay compound interest,

900
00:29:23,841 --> 00:29:25,756
usually daily or monthly.

901
00:29:25,936 --> 00:29:28,725
But rates on many traditional savings accounts

902
00:29:28,725 --> 00:29:29,210
are low.

903
00:29:29,390 --> 00:29:32,142
High yield savings accounts usually pay more.

904
00:29:32,492 --> 00:29:33,444
Question two.

905
00:29:33,624 --> 00:29:34,878
Do stocks compound?

906
00:29:35,058 --> 00:29:37,375
Stocks don't pay a set interest rate,

907
00:29:37,375 --> 00:29:40,319
but they compound through rising company values

908
00:29:40,319 --> 00:29:41,884
and reinvested dividends.

909
00:29:42,064 --> 00:29:44,619
That's why long term stock investing is

910
00:29:44,619 --> 00:29:46,650
often described as compounding.

911
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:48,056
Question three.

912
00:29:48,236 --> 00:29:49,945
Is it too late to benefit from

913
00:29:49,945 --> 00:29:50,628
compounding?

914
00:29:50,808 --> 00:29:50,994
No.

915
00:29:51,174 --> 00:29:53,549
Even ten or twenty years of compounding

916
00:29:53,549 --> 00:29:55,133
can make a big difference.

917
00:29:55,313 --> 00:29:57,378
Start now and invest as much as

918
00:29:57,378 --> 00:29:58,645
you reasonably can.

919
00:29:58,995 --> 00:30:00,016
Question four.

920
00:30:00,196 --> 00:30:02,600
Does inflation cancel out compounding?

921
00:30:02,780 --> 00:30:03,964
It works against it.

922
00:30:04,144 --> 00:30:07,470
Inflation slowly shrinks what each dollar can

923
00:30:07,470 --> 00:30:07,766
buy.

924
00:30:07,946 --> 00:30:09,556
At three percent inflation,

925
00:30:09,556 --> 00:30:12,120
five thousand dollars twenty years from now

926
00:30:12,120 --> 00:30:14,743
buys roughly what two thousand eight hundred

927
00:30:14,743 --> 00:30:15,876
dollars buys today.

928
00:30:16,056 --> 00:30:17,933
That's why money that must grow for

929
00:30:17,933 --> 00:30:20,024
decades usually needs to earn more than

930
00:30:20,024 --> 00:30:20,560
inflation.

931
00:30:20,911 --> 00:30:21,909
Question five.

932
00:30:22,089 --> 00:30:24,899
What's the difference between APR and APY?

933
00:30:25,079 --> 00:30:28,402
APR is the yearly rate before compounding

934
00:30:28,402 --> 00:30:29,293
is counted.

935
00:30:29,473 --> 00:30:32,063
APY, the annual percentage yield,

936
00:30:32,063 --> 00:30:33,711
includes compounding.

937
00:30:33,891 --> 00:30:35,788
So when you're saving,

938
00:30:35,788 --> 00:30:36,909
compare APYs.

939
00:30:37,089 --> 00:30:38,671
And when you're borrowing,

940
00:30:38,671 --> 00:30:40,618
remember that the true cost of a

941
00:30:40,618 --> 00:30:42,747
card with a twenty four percent APR

942
00:30:42,747 --> 00:30:45,180
is even a little higher once compounding

943
00:30:45,180 --> 00:30:45,727
kicks in.

944
00:30:46,077 --> 00:30:47,214
Question six.

945
00:30:47,394 --> 00:30:49,327
Should I pay off my mortgage or

946
00:30:49,327 --> 00:30:49,763
invest?

947
00:30:49,943 --> 00:30:51,351
It depends on your rate,

948
00:30:51,351 --> 00:30:53,345
your taxes and your peace of mind.

949
00:30:53,525 --> 00:30:55,889
Generally, the higher your mortgage rate,

950
00:30:55,889 --> 00:30:57,793
the more paying it down acts like

951
00:30:57,793 --> 00:30:58,946
a guaranteed return.

952
00:30:59,126 --> 00:31:00,496
Many people do both.

953
00:31:00,676 --> 00:31:02,885
A low rate mortgage is very different

954
00:31:02,885 --> 00:31:04,914
from a twenty percent credit card.

955
00:31:05,264 --> 00:31:06,378
Question seven.

956
00:31:06,558 --> 00:31:08,602
How do I know my real return?

957
00:31:08,782 --> 00:31:11,126
Most brokerages show a personal rate of

958
00:31:11,126 --> 00:31:12,508
return in your account.

959
00:31:12,688 --> 00:31:13,444
Look for it,

960
00:31:13,444 --> 00:31:15,458
and compare it to a simple broad

961
00:31:15,458 --> 00:31:17,472
index fund over the same period.

962
00:31:17,652 --> 00:31:19,201
If you're far behind,

963
00:31:19,201 --> 00:31:21,635
high fees or too much trading may

964
00:31:21,635 --> 00:31:22,667
be the reason.

965
00:31:23,017 --> 00:31:23,876
Question eight.

966
00:31:24,056 --> 00:31:26,877
Can compounding work with very small amounts?

967
00:31:27,057 --> 00:31:27,986
Absolutely.

968
00:31:28,166 --> 00:31:30,392
Twenty five dollars a month won't make

969
00:31:30,392 --> 00:31:31,564
you rich on its own,

970
00:31:31,564 --> 00:31:32,970
but it builds the habit,

971
00:31:32,970 --> 00:31:34,435
and habits are what grow.

972
00:31:34,615 --> 00:31:36,762
Many people who start tiny raise the

973
00:31:36,762 --> 00:31:39,027
amount over the years as income grows.

974
00:31:39,207 --> 00:31:41,012
The habit you build at twenty five

975
00:31:41,012 --> 00:31:42,924
dollars is the same habit that later

976
00:31:42,924 --> 00:31:43,932
moves two thousand.

977
00:31:44,283 --> 00:31:45,177
Question nine.

978
00:31:45,357 --> 00:31:47,476
What if the market crashes right after

979
00:31:47,476 --> 00:31:47,922
I start?

980
00:31:48,102 --> 00:31:50,378
Crashes happen, and they're painful.

981
00:31:50,558 --> 00:31:52,759
But for someone investing every month,

982
00:31:52,759 --> 00:31:55,134
a falling market means buying more shares

983
00:31:55,134 --> 00:31:56,061
at lower prices.

984
00:31:56,241 --> 00:31:59,633
Historically, broad markets have recovered from past

985
00:31:59,633 --> 00:32:00,807
crashes over time,

986
00:32:00,807 --> 00:32:02,960
though there's never a guarantee,

987
00:32:02,960 --> 00:32:04,786
and recovery can take years.

988
00:32:05,136 --> 00:32:06,134
Mistake one.

989
00:32:06,314 --> 00:32:08,683
Keeping long term money in cash for

990
00:32:08,683 --> 00:32:09,089
years.

991
00:32:09,269 --> 00:32:11,772
Cash is great for an emergency fund

992
00:32:11,772 --> 00:32:13,274
and short term goals.

993
00:32:13,454 --> 00:32:15,636
But money you won't need for decades,

994
00:32:15,636 --> 00:32:17,877
sitting in an account earning far less

995
00:32:17,877 --> 00:32:20,648
than inflation, loses buying power every single

996
00:32:20,648 --> 00:32:20,943
year.

997
00:32:21,123 --> 00:32:22,604
Know which money is for safety,

998
00:32:22,604 --> 00:32:24,037
and which money is for growth.

999
00:32:24,387 --> 00:32:25,606
Mistake two.

1000
00:32:25,786 --> 00:32:27,307
Waiting for the perfect moment.

1001
00:32:27,487 --> 00:32:29,722
People wait for the market to drop,

1002
00:32:29,722 --> 00:32:30,488
for a raise,

1003
00:32:30,488 --> 00:32:31,573
for the new year,

1004
00:32:31,573 --> 00:32:32,595
for less stress.

1005
00:32:32,775 --> 00:32:35,190
Meanwhile, months turn into years.

1006
00:32:35,370 --> 00:32:37,802
Nobody can reliably predict the market,

1007
00:32:37,802 --> 00:32:40,734
and the perfect moment rarely announces itself.

1008
00:32:40,914 --> 00:32:43,521
A steady monthly habit removes the guessing

1009
00:32:43,521 --> 00:32:44,188
completely.

1010
00:32:44,538 --> 00:32:45,652
Mistake three.

1011
00:32:45,832 --> 00:32:47,539
Ignoring the reverse snowball.

1012
00:32:47,719 --> 00:32:51,200
Some people invest diligently while carrying credit

1013
00:32:51,200 --> 00:32:53,930
card balances at twenty percent or more.

1014
00:32:54,110 --> 00:32:56,367
The debt often compounds faster than the

1015
00:32:56,367 --> 00:32:57,326
investments grow.

1016
00:32:57,506 --> 00:32:59,297
Fixing that first is one of the

1017
00:32:59,297 --> 00:33:01,722
highest return moves most people will ever

1018
00:33:01,722 --> 00:33:02,011
make.

1019
00:33:02,361 --> 00:33:03,603
Avoid those three,

1020
00:33:03,603 --> 00:33:04,984
follow the playbook,

1021
00:33:04,984 --> 00:33:06,157
and give it time.

1022
00:33:06,337 --> 00:33:08,879
That's honestly most of what compounding asks

1023
00:33:08,879 --> 00:33:09,275
of you.

1024
00:33:09,455 --> 00:33:11,549
It's simple, but it isn't easy,

1025
00:33:11,549 --> 00:33:13,779
because it asks for patience in a

1026
00:33:13,779 --> 00:33:15,468
world that rewards speed.

1027
00:33:15,819 --> 00:33:16,573
Quick recap.

1028
00:33:16,753 --> 00:33:18,599
Angle one, the numbers.

1029
00:33:18,779 --> 00:33:20,538
Simple versus compound,

1030
00:33:20,538 --> 00:33:22,373
the rule of seventy two,

1031
00:33:22,373 --> 00:33:25,813
compounding frequency, monthly contributions,

1032
00:33:25,813 --> 00:33:29,330
Franklin's experiment, Buffett's late fortune,

1033
00:33:29,330 --> 00:33:30,935
and compounding debt.

1034
00:33:31,285 --> 00:33:33,200
Angle two, the psychology.

1035
00:33:33,380 --> 00:33:35,176
Exponential growth bias,

1036
00:33:35,176 --> 00:33:36,523
the boring middle,

1037
00:33:36,523 --> 00:33:38,393
impatience, present bias,

1038
00:33:38,393 --> 00:33:40,788
interruptions, the literacy gap,

1039
00:33:40,788 --> 00:33:42,808
and small rate differences.

1040
00:33:43,158 --> 00:33:44,922
Angle three, the playbook.

1041
00:33:45,102 --> 00:33:47,637
Start now, kill compounding debt,

1042
00:33:47,637 --> 00:33:50,556
reinvest everything, lower your costs,

1043
00:33:50,556 --> 00:33:52,092
automate and ignore,

1044
00:33:52,092 --> 00:33:54,089
and compound yourself too.

1045
00:33:54,439 --> 00:33:55,931
Whoever said it first,

1046
00:33:55,931 --> 00:33:56,610
it's true.

1047
00:33:56,790 --> 00:33:59,050
He who understands compound interest,

1048
00:33:59,050 --> 00:33:59,599
earns it.

1049
00:33:59,779 --> 00:34:00,794
He who doesn't,

1050
00:34:00,794 --> 00:34:01,335
pays it.

1051
00:34:01,515 --> 00:34:02,723
Now you understand it.

1052
00:34:02,903 --> 00:34:04,504
If you want the full plan for

1053
00:34:04,504 --> 00:34:05,553
putting it to work,

1054
00:34:05,553 --> 00:34:06,657
it's in my playbook,

1055
00:34:06,657 --> 00:34:08,092
linked in the description.

1056
00:34:08,272 --> 00:34:09,758
Tell me in the comments,

1057
00:34:09,758 --> 00:34:12,173
what's your rule of seventy two number?

1058
00:34:12,353 --> 00:34:13,491
I'm Ryan Rover.

1059
00:34:13,671 --> 00:34:14,890
See you in the next one.
